{
  "rule": "What each TypeScript compiler node kind is expected to be in our tree. A boundary comparison asks whether we have a node at these bytes; this asks whether it is the RIGHT node, which is the half a boundary check cannot see -- `foo();` parsed as a bodyless function declaration occupies exactly the bytes of the call it should be.",
  "semantics": "Several of our nodes can cover one span, so an oracle node passes when ANY of our kinds at its span is listed. Only the CORE kind needs an entry; wrappers that share the span come along free. The mapping is not one-to-one and is not required to be -- it is required to be TOTAL and DECLARED, so anything not listed is reported rather than assumed.",
  "modifiers_note": "tsc keeps modifiers INSIDE the declaration they modify, so a ClassDeclaration's span starts at `export`. We wrap the declaration in an `export_statement` instead. That is why every exportable declaration lists `export_statement` alongside its own kind.",
  "tokens_note": "Entries whose oracle kind ends in Token or Keyword were generated from the corpus, not written by hand: `AmpersandAmpersandToken` is `&&` and there is no judgement in saying so. They are kept rather than skipped because tokenising the same run of bytes is worth checking -- `&&` read as two `&` would show up here.",
